WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.260
What lives under a rock in a shallow creek?

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.280
Lift one, and tiny animals scramble into view.

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.620
You and your child will look closely, sort what you find, and check a guess

4
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:12.620
together.

5
00:00:13.083 --> 00:00:16.063
You do not need a science background to lead this trip.

6
00:00:17.303 --> 00:00:22.363
Your child practices slowing down, looking closely at tiny clues, and sorting what

7
00:00:22.363 --> 00:00:28.183
they find. They also practice making a guess and checking it against real evidence.

8
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:34.563
Pack a kitchen strainer, a white plastic tray, a plastic spoon, and closed-toe water

9
00:00:34.563 --> 00:00:40.743
shoes. Bring soap, clean water, sunscreen, insect repellent, and a clear plastic

10
00:00:40.743 --> 00:00:46.383
cup. Choose a shallow, slow riffle in a public park where collecting is allowed.

11
00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:49.323
Check posted water advisories.

12
00:00:50.663 --> 00:00:54.583
Go when water is low and clear, at least three days after heavy rain.

13
00:00:55.717 --> 00:00:58.697
Stay within arm’s reach in or beside the water.

14
00:01:00.117 --> 00:01:03.197
Children under five stay on the bank with a second adult.

15
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:07.777
Wade only in slow, clear water up to your child’s ankles.

16
00:01:09.157 --> 00:01:11.997
Wear closed-toe shoes for slippery rocks and hidden glass.

17
00:01:13.377 --> 00:01:17.417
Never drink creek water or taste wild plants, berries, or mushrooms.

18
00:01:18.837 --> 00:01:22.777
Keep tiny animals and small objects away from children under four.

19
00:01:22.777 --> 00:01:25.497
Call nine one one if choking.

20
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.500
Fill the shaded tray with two to three centimeters of creek water.

21
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.440
Ask your child, "What do you think lives under this rock?"

22
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.540
Look for snakes before reaching.

23
00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.920
Lift the rock yourself and check underneath.

24
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.080
Gently put the rock back if you see a crayfish, hellgrammite, or another animal that

25
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.820
can pinch, bite, or sting.

26
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.380
At a safe rock, have your wading child hold the strainer flat just downstream.

27
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.920
Then gently rub the underside for about thirty seconds.

28
00:01:58.983 --> 00:02:00.943
Tip the strainer into the white tray.

29
00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:05.503
Try three or four rocks, then give the mud a minute to settle.

30
00:02:06.923 --> 00:02:08.543
Watch for specks that crawl or dart.

31
00:02:09.923 --> 00:02:11.903
Ask, "Why do you think they hide under rocks?"

32
00:02:12.663 --> 00:02:17.643
Use the spoon, never fingers, to sort crawlers, swimmers, and animals that stay

33
00:02:17.643 --> 00:02:18.643
still.

34
00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:21.493
You have sorted the animals by how they move.

35
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:24.153
Now look at which kinds you found.

36
00:02:25.493 --> 00:02:30.193
If your tray held several kinds of mayfly and stonefly young, what might that

37
00:02:30.193 --> 00:02:31.373
suggest about the water?

38
00:02:36.967 --> 00:02:39.007
Several kinds can be a good sign.

39
00:02:40.387 --> 00:02:44.967
Most stonefly young and many mayfly young need cool, clean water with plenty of

40
00:02:44.967 --> 00:02:49.647
oxygen. Mayfly young usually have three tails and side gills.

41
00:02:51.107 --> 00:02:52.487
Stonefly young have two tails.

42
00:02:53.927 --> 00:02:56.667
Caddisfly young often live in sand or stick cases.

43
00:02:58.087 --> 00:03:02.907
Mostly worms, leeches, or tiny red larvae may suggest less healthy water.

44
00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:09.143
A creek is a connected food web, and its hidden animals give clues to water health.

45
00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:13.203
Ask, "What do you think this one eats, and what might eat it?"

46
00:03:13.763 --> 00:03:15.603
Many eat leaf bits, and fish eat them.

47
00:03:16.803 --> 00:03:19.183
One visit is a clue, not a final answer.

48
00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:24.283
Pour the tray back slowly at the water’s edge near the rocks you sampled.

49
00:03:25.703 --> 00:03:27.263
Set each rock back as it was.

50
00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:30.383
Empty any containers before leaving.

51
00:03:31.783 --> 00:03:36.003
Wash hands with soap and water, wash the gear, and check everyone for ticks.

52
00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:39.843
The mix you found gives you a clue about the creek.
